          "session_id": "full-g-md-001",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My home blood pressure has been around 150/95 for two weeks. Is that something I need to act on now, and what do the guidelines say the target should be?",
          "answer": "Your home blood pressure readings of 150/95 sustained over two weeks suggest that you should discuss this with a clinician.\n\nThese readings fall into the category of \"Stage 2 hypertension\" according to United States society guidance, which defines this as a systolic pressure of at least 140 mm Hg or a diastolic pressure of at least 90 mm Hg (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets). Major cardiology society consensus guidance also suggests Stage 2 hypertension for these readings, applying when the systolic pressure is at least 140 mm Hg or the diastolic pressure is at least 90 mm Hg (\"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets,\" Major cardiology society consensus guidance). A clinician would assess your blood pressure through repeated measurements to make a diagnosis (condition-hypertension).\n\nFor most people, clinical guidance recommends a blood pressure target below 130/80 mm Hg (\"Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis,\" Major cardiology society consensus guidance). However, other provided guidelines do not state a universal number, noting that the exact numerical target can vary depending on individual factors (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets). Your clinician would establish a personalized target for you and review it periodically (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
